{{define "content"}}
<h1>cRust</h1>
<p class="lede">An interpreted language where every keyword is pizza jargon —
built to solve <a href="https://adventofcode.com/">Advent of Code</a> without
losing the fun of the syntax along the way.</p>

<div class="card">
  <h3>A taste</h3>
  <pre><code>recipe findPair(nums, target) {
    knead (i = 0; i &lt; slices(nums); i++) {
        knead (j = i + 1; j &lt; slices(nums); j++) {
            order (nums[i] + nums[j] == target) {
                serve [nums[i], nums[j]]
            }
        }
    }
    serve nobox
}</code></pre>
</div>

<h2>What's here</h2>
<p>This site is generated by <code>crust bake documentation</code> and reads
its reference material straight from the same tables the interpreter, the
language server, and <code>crust develop</code> use — so <a href="/keywords">Keywords</a>
and <a href="/builtins">Standard Library</a> below are never out of date
with what your build of <code>crust</code> actually understands.</p>

<ul>
  <li><a href="/getting-started">Getting Started</a> — build it, run your first file</li>
  <li><a href="/keywords">Keywords</a> — the full reserved-word vocabulary, one entry per keyword</li>
  <li><a href="/builtins">Standard Library</a> — every builtin function, live from this binary</li>
  <li><a href="/grammar">Syntax Reference</a> — operators, types, and control flow at a glance</li>
  <li><a href="/examples">Examples</a> — a couple of runnable AoC-shaped snippets</li>
</ul>

<h2>No `let`, no ceremony</h2>
<p>Variables just get assigned, Python-style. cRust is a small, tree-walking
interpreter with just enough — ints, floats, strings, lists, maps, sets,
functions, control flow — to comfortably solve AoC-style puzzles, wrapped in
a pizzeria-themed syntax.</p>
